<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajaraja I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/985">985</a>–<a href="/years/1014">1014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Rajaraja Chola I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajaraja I was emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, reigning from 985 to 1014. He conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> and Chera lands, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, where he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> in 993, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a>, and he ended Western Ganga rule at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talakad">Talakad</a>. He built the Brihadishvara temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and organised a strong navy and a survey of land revenue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i.jpg" alt="Rajaraja I" width="320" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Venu62 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raraja_detail.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rajaraja was born around 947, and the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> recognises 3 November 947 as his birthdate. His birth name was Arulmozhi Varman. He was the son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king Parantaka II, also called Sundara Chola, and Vanavan Mahadevi of the Malaiyaman-Pari family, with an elder brother, Aditha II, and an elder sister, Kundavai. Aditha II died in mysterious circumstances, and inscriptions suggest he may have been assassinated. After Sundara Chola's death, Madhurantakan took the throne as Uttama Chola. When he died, Arulmozhi Varman became king in mid-985 and took the name Rajaraja, meaning King among Kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rajaraja inherited a small kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a> that was recovering from attacks by the Rashtrakuta Empire, and he made it a well-organised empire with a powerful army and navy. Around 988 he destroyed Kandalur Salai in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a>, and his inscriptions in Kerala and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> country show the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kollam">Kollam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodungallur">Kodungallur</a>. He took the title Mummudi Chola, three-crowned, for rule over the Cholas, Pandyas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Cheras</a>. In 993 he conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>, and the Cholas made northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> a province, with a capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a>, which they renamed Jananathamangalam. A naval campaign brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maldives">Maldives</a> under Chola control.</p><p>In the 990s and 1000s Rajaraja annexed Gangapadi, Nolambapadi and Tadigaipadi and fought the Western Chalukyas under Satyashraya. In the east he defeated the Telugu Chola king Jata Choda Bhima over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a> and arranged the marriage of his daughter Kundavai to the Eastern Chalukya prince Vimaladitya. In 1000 he began a land survey and assessment that reorganised the country into units called valanadus, and hereditary lords were replaced or made dependent officials. He also sent the first Chola mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. A follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> who also dedicated temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, he called himself Shivapada Shekhara. In 1010 he built the Peruvudaiyar Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Thanjavur</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> site Great Living Chola Temples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rajaraja is credited with gathering the hymns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appar">Appar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambandar">Sambandar</a> and Sundarar, with the help of Nambiyandar Nambi, into the collection known as the Thirumurai, and was called Thirumurai Kanda Cholar. His coins showed the standing king on one side and a seated goddess on the other, and were copied by Sri Lankan kings. Near the end of his reign he made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-i">Rajendra Chola I</a> co-regent. Rajaraja died in 1014 and Rajendra succeeded him, and went on to lead the Chola invasion of Srivijaya.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajaraja I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under <b>Rajaraja I</b> and his successors the empire became a military power in South and Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that <b>Rajaraja I</b> of the Chola dynasty (985–1013 CE), hearing short excerpts at his court, set out to recover them, and that the priest Nambiyandar Nambi found the palm leaves, half eaten by white ants, in a chamber of the Chidambaram Nataraja temple.</blockquote><a href="/songs/tevaram">Tevaram</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chola Empire under <b>Rajaraja I</b> took Anuradhapura in 993, and in 1017 Rajendra I captured King Mahinda V. The Sinhalese capital later moved to Polonnaruwa, beginning the Polonnaruwa Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Rajaraja I</b> of the Chola Empire captured Talakad around 1000, ending Ganga rule; Gangavadi later passed to the Hoysala Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Ganga dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
